course: dictionary
lesson: 12154
title: Competent: able, and able by law
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Competent means able to do something well enough, or properly. If someone is a competent swimmer, they can swim safely and well, even if they are not the fastest. «Maya is a competent reader for her age» means she reads well enough for her age.
explain: Competent is an adjective, so it describes a person or thing. Its more form is «more competent», as in «Sam is more competent at maths than he was last year.» When you meet it, look at the words around it to see what someone can do.
explain: Competent has a second meaning, used in law. It means allowed or able by law to decide or act. «The court is competent to hear this case» means the court has the legal power to hear it. Ask yourself: is this sentence about skill, or about the law?
fact: Competent means able to do something well enough or properly.
fact: Competent is an adjective. Its more form is «more competent».
fact: «She is a competent driver» means she can drive safely and properly.
fact: In law, competent means allowed or able by law to decide or act.
fact: Competent is not the same as excellent: it means good enough to do the job well.
exercise: «Ella is a competent swimmer, so she can swim safely in the sea.» What does competent mean here? | *Able to do it well enough | Lost in the water | Scared of the sea | Very tired
exercise: «The pilot was competent and calm during the storm.» Find and copy one word that means able to do something well enough. | during | storm | *competent | calm
exercise: In court, the judge is competent to decide the case. What does competent mean in law? | *Allowed by law to decide | Lost in the building | Very rude to the people in court | Able to cook dinner
test: «Sam is a competent reader, so he can read the whole book alone.» What does competent mean? | Reading very slowly | Too young to read | *Able to read well enough | Bored of books
test: «The court is competent to hear this case.» In this sentence, what does competent mean? | Angry with the judge | *Allowed by law to hear it | Unable to speak | Good at singing
exam: «Maya is more competent at maths than she was last year.» What does more competent suggest? | Scared of numbers | *Better able to do maths well enough | Worse at maths | Finished with maths
